'''Capitoline Hill''' ({{lang-bl|Turó Capitolino}}), is a densely populated [[Autonomous commune (Balisca)|autonomous commune]] and barrio within the [[Castejón Metropolis]], named after the {{W|Capitoline Hill|eponymous ancient hill in the Italian capital}}. It is also commonly known as ''Capitolino''. The barrio is known as one of the main centers of the [[Desconexión|''desconexión'']] movement, and is well known for it's widespread localist and [[W:Direct action|direct activism]]. It is the Baliscan commune with the highest percentage of English speakers − 93% of the population was able to speak the language in 2025.

The neighborhood was transferred from the government of [[Araucaria]] to that of the [[Castejón]] following the narrow approval (50.8%) of the annexation in the [[1975 Trinacria referendum]], and was subsequently incorporated as the borough of 12th borough of Castejón. It is the home to the [[University of Castejón, Capitoline]]. During the [[2021 Baliscan civil unrest]], militant activists drove the [[Mozos de Escuadra]] out of the barrio following their attempts to enforce curfews, and declared an [[Autonomous commune|Autonomous Zone]].

Following lengthy [[Reconciliation council (Balisca)|legal battles]], the cantonal government began treating autonomous Capitoline Hill as a legally self-governing community. The [[Constitution of Balisca|Constitution of 2022]] officially permitted self government and un-armed self-policing by local communities, being legitimized by the [[Thirty-sixth Amendment to the Baliscan Constitution|36th Amendment]]. Capitoline Hill subsequently became the first autonomous commune to be recognized.

